pricing_tool_selection::challenger::v1.text:
{
  "findings": [
    {
      "question": "claims_supported",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "Most claims resolve, but two load-bearing ones do not. (1) C8 is labelled a fact: 'Execution improvement in the 121 stores is concentrated in the commuter and high street formats.' No evidence records the format composition of the 121 sub-40% stores. The only record of them is a note that the spread is wide; the format rows are averages over 96/118/198 stores, and neighbourhood alone holds 198 stores at an 84.6% mean, which is fully compatible with a long low tail. The draft's own 'what would change it' concedes the per-store picture is unknown, so C8 is an inference dressed as a finding. (2) ALT-04's strategic_alignment asserts 'the availability floor is not put at risk by design' because ordering is unchanged. Pulling final-day reductions earlier accelerates sell-through of exactly the stock measured at 17:00; nothing in evidence supports the no-risk assertion, and the measure is already below floor. Also minor: C5 asserts a contradiction of the Retail Director but cites only the metric row and the objectives, not the block containing the statement.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-72006df6",
          "quote": "compliance spread is wide: 137 stores are above 90 percent and 121 stores are below 40 percent"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-51db2484",
          "quote": "stores: 198",
          "locator": "neighbourhood 2026-Q2 row"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-1eef13f9",
          "quote": "value: 92.6",
          "locator": "on_shelf_availability_1700, 2026-Q2"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-d6d5dcd4",
          "quote": "Availability has never dropped below the 93% floor."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "contradictions_considered",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "The vendor, availability, baseline and trial-attribution conflicts are engaged well. Two are handled loosely. First, the redistribution-partnership confound is cited as weakening the compliance/loss pattern but its direction is never worked out: diverted volume is booked as waste rather than markdown in 68 neighbourhood stores, which depresses neighbourhood markdown loss and inflates its waste. That specifically undercuts the second leg of the support rationale — that the highest-compliance format also carries the lowest waste — and it is quoted as reassurance rather than analysed. Second, the trend data are not engaged at all: between 2025-Q2 and 2026-Q2 markdown loss rose in every format while ESL was zero everywhere in the earlier period, so the ESL-concentration confound is weaker than stated, but the parallel rise in a high-compliance format (neighbourhood 86.1%→84.6% compliance, 3.4%→3.9% loss) suggests a common driver other than execution timing. That is the single most direct challenge to ALT-04's premise and it is absent.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-883d223e",
          "quote": "A food redistribution partnership operates in 68 neighbourhood stores. Collections are made at close and the diverted volume is recorded as waste, not as markdown."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-cb10d2dd",
          "quote": "markdown_compliance_pct: 86.1",
          "locator": "neighbourhood 2025-Q2"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-cb10d2dd",
          "quote": "markdown_loss_rate_pct: 3.4",
          "locator": "neighbourhood 2025-Q2"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-20c2fe70",
          "quote": "esl_equipped_stores: 0",
          "locator": "high street 2025-Q2"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "preference_as_evidence",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "The draft is disciplined about the Retail Director, Head of Data, Finance and Regional Manager — all correctly marked as opinion. But C3, the claim that timing improvement will not push loss into waste, is labelled a fact and rests entirely on a category-management assertion of mechanism in a strategy document, not on any measurement at Larkfield. It is the whole basis of the 'less likely than depth or timing changes to push loss into waste' argument in the support rationale, and it is a stated rationale carried as a finding. The converse of the document's statement (that earlier execution lowers both) is also not what the document says.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-883d223e",
          "quote": "Late execution therefore raises markdown loss and waste together rather than trading one against the other."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-59823a34",
          "quote": "never populated; the till does not distinguish a marked-down sale from a full-price sale"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "non_ai_considered",
      "verdict": "passes",
      "explanation": "The non-AI options are genuinely worked, not listed. ALT-05, ALT-06, ALT-08 and ALT-09 each carry specific evidence and specific defects (no conversion measure to size training, price-integrity sequencing risk on the nightly file, the Ashcombe shelf-life day landing in April 2026 while salads still ran 9.6% in Q2). The selected option is itself a non-AI process change, and the AI options are refused on documented technical grounds rather than rhetoric. One gap worth noting rather than penalising: ALT-09 is not tested against the largest pound-loss segment, sandwiches and food to go at 6.4% on 27% of fresh sales, where a one-day shelf life is the binding constraint.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-dae3952a",
          "quote": "value: 6.4",
          "locator": "sandwiches_and_food_to_go markdown_loss_rate 2026-Q2"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-a2a9873c",
          "quote": "value: 27.0",
          "locator": "sandwiches_and_food_to_go share of fresh sales"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "no_build_considered",
      "verdict": "passes",
      "explanation": "ALT-01 (hold), ALT-02 (defer pricing) and ALT-03 (controlled trial first) are each argued on their merits, and ALT-03 is explicitly named as the fallback if an unsized effect is unacceptable. The draft also concedes the honest version of the no-build case: no unit economics, no labour measurement, and no attribution path until the till flag exists. The remaining weakness is that the recommendation as conditioned — randomised, matched-control, pre-registered read window, hours instrumented as a stop condition — is very nearly ALT-03 with an active arm, so the claim that ALT-04 'can plausibly move markdown loss inside FY26' should be read as applying to 121 stores under trial conditions, not the estate.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-e27078c1",
          "quote": "Markdown loss in the trial stores was not reported separately."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-935708d5",
          "quote": "Markdown and waste records reach the analytics warehouse on a T+2 schedule."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "overconfident",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "Moderate is above what the evidence earns, and the draft's own caveats say why. The causal chain is unmeasured at every link: the target population's loss profile is unknown (C8 is inference), the only direct observation of late starts is six regional-manager-nominated stores for one day each, no colleague-hours figure exists for any intervention, and no unit economics exist to size a point of markdown against a point of waste. Worse, the mechanism may run against the claim: compliance is defined as the share of final-day items carrying a reduction label before 18:00, so raising compliance means marking down more items and marking them down sooner, which mechanically increases markdown loss on units that would otherwise have sold at full price. The cross-format correlation cannot distinguish that from the assumed effect, and there is no conversion measure to detect it. The draft also states ALT-04 requires 'no policy change to D-2025-11' while its own test starts the round before 17:00, which is a change to the standardised 17:00 round and may reopen both the decision and the forum question. Support should read low.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-883d223e",
          "quote": "Markdown compliance is defined as the share of items reaching their final day that carried a reduction label before 18:00."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-2a2e9b15",
          "quote": "standardise markdown on one 17:00 round across all formats."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-e27078c1",
          "quote": "The six stores were nominated by the regional manager. They were not selected at random."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-52c4f4f2",
          "quote": "Any system that issues work instructions to store colleagues requires consultation with the colleague forum before deployment."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "what_could_make_it_wrong",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "Four specific ways this is a mistake, not generic risk. (1) Direction reversal: because compliance counts items reduced before 18:00, recovering the 121 stores increases the number and earliness of reductions. Markdown loss could rise while waste falls — the exact opposite of the stated outcome — and with no marked-down-sale flag and T+2 reporting the trial cannot tell margin recovered from margin given away. (2) Population error: if the 121 stores are disproportionately small neighbourhood sites whose loss is already near 3.9%, the addressable loss is small and the option has no premise. (3) Availability: 17:00 availability is already 92.6%, below the 93% floor required in every format, and no per-format rows exist. Pulling final-day sell-through earlier is the most plausible way to push that further down precisely in the treated stores. (4) Governance: if operations or the forum rule that a regional-operations instruction to change the shift routine is a work instruction to colleagues, the six-to-twelve-week path applies and the entire FY26 timing advantage over ALT-07/ALT-08 disappears. The draft lists (4) as a condition but treats a favourable ruling as the base case.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-59823a34",
          "quote": "never populated; the till does not distinguish a marked-down sale from a full-price sale"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-51db2484",
          "quote": "markdown_loss_rate_pct: 3.9",
          "locator": "neighbourhood 2026-Q2"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-daadf395",
          "quote": "Hold on-shelf availability at 17:00 at or above 93% in every format."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-935708d5",
          "quote": "Each store shares one handheld device between the colleagues on shift."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "what_to_test",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "The proposed test is reasonable in design but is scheduled to spend operations effort before two free checks have been done, and it omits the measure that would detect the failure mode above. Before any rollout effort is committed, pull the existing per-store export for the 121 stores (markdown loss, waste, category mix, format, delivery window) — this exists in the same source as the compliance figure and can kill or confirm the premise at no cost. Then confirm the forum scope ruling in writing, since an adverse ruling removes the option's only delivery advantage. In the trial itself, markdown loss and waste must be read as separate lines — the March-May 2026 trial failed precisely because markdown loss was never reported separately — and colleague hours per store week must be instrumented from week one rather than reported at the end. Availability at 17:00 must be read per treated store, not chain-level, given the floor is already breached. The till marked-down-sale flag needs an engineering estimate before it is committed from the same envelope; none exists in evidence.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-e27078c1",
          "quote": "No control stores were used."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-e27078c1",
          "quote": "Markdown loss in the trial stores was not reported separately."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-2a2e9b15",
          "quote": "fund the fresh loss programme at £180,000 of revenue expenditure for FY26. No capital allocated."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "reclassify": [
    {
      "claim_id": "C8",
      "new_type": "assumption",
      "reason": "Labelled fact, but no evidence records the format composition or loss profile of the 121 sub-40% stores. It is inferred from format averages over 96/118/198 stores, and the draft's own 'what would change it' lists that breakdown as missing."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "C3",
      "new_type": "assumption",
      "reason": "Labelled fact, but it is a mechanism assertion in the category review document with no Larkfield measurement behind it; markdown-to-sale conversion has never been populated, so the markdown/waste relationship cannot be observed. It is also the load-bearing reason for believing loss will not move into waste."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "C15",
      "new_type": "assumption",
      "reason": "The governance text supports the six-to-twelve-week and eight-to-ten-week requirements, but the conclusion that 'an operations routine change avoids both' is the drafter's reading of scope, not a recorded constraint. The recommendation itself makes confirming it a condition, so it should not be carried as a governance_constraint."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "C2",
      "new_type": "assumption",
      "reason": "The individual format numbers are facts, but the claim as written ('higher compliance coincides with lower markdown loss and lower waste') is a cross-format inference that the same evidence set shows is confounded by the 68-store redistribution partnership booking diverted volume as waste rather than markdown."
    }
  ],
  "recommended_support": "low",
  "what_would_change_it": [
    "Per-store markdown loss, waste, category mix, format and delivery window for the 121 stores below 40% compliance. If their loss sits near the high-compliance stores, ALT-04 has no premise and the option should be withdrawn, not merely downgraded.",
    "A decomposition showing whether higher compliance lowers total loss or merely converts waste into markdown. Because compliance counts items reduced before 18:00, raising it necessarily marks down more items earlier; if the net effect on markdown loss is positive, the recommendation targets the wrong direction on the primary metric.",
    "A written forum scope ruling on whether a regional-operations shift-routine change is a work instruction to colleagues. An adverse ruling removes the six-to-twelve-week advantage that is the stated reason for preferring ALT-04 over ALT-07 and ALT-08.",
    "17:00 availability by format and for the treated stores. The chain figure is already 92.6% against a 93% floor required in every format, and earlier markdown is a plausible way to depress it further where the option is applied.",
    "Measured colleague hours per store week for the reworked routine, against the 6.8 hours already spent in high street versus 4.1 in neighbourhood. If earlier starts add or displace hours, the labour cap is breached.",
    "Cash value of a point of markdown loss and a point of waste, plus the fresh sales base by format, without which ALT-04 cannot be ranked against ALT-08 or ALT-09 at all.",
    "Whether starting the round before 17:00 is a variation of D-2025-11. If it is, the claim of 'no policy change' fails and the decision needs re-opening rather than a regional instruction."
  ],
  "what_to_test": [
    "Before committing operations effort: pull the existing store execution export at store level for the 121 sub-40% stores and confirm their loss rate exceeds the high-compliance cohort. This is a free query against a source already in evidence and can invalidate the premise.",
    "Obtain and record the colleague forum scope ruling and the operations view on D-2025-11 variation, both before launch, not during.",
    "Get an engineering effort and cost estimate for the till marked-down-sale flag before allocating any of the £180,000 to it; none exists in evidence.",
    "In the trial: report markdown loss and waste as separate lines per arm, with a pre-registered read window allowing the T+2 lag, stratified by format and delivery window so the July-September prepared-salads peak cannot read as an effect.",
    "Instrument colleague hours per store week and 17:00 availability per treated store weekly, with any hours increase or availability fall below 93% as a pre-declared stop condition.",
    "Freeze supplier shelf-life, specification and refit changes in trial and control stores across the read window; the concurrent Ashcombe change and two refits are what made the March-May 2026 trial uninterpretable.",
    "Explicitly test whether a share of the compliance gain shows up as full-price sales lost rather than waste avoided, using whatever partial conversion signal the till flag can deliver once built."
  ]
}

pricing_tool_selection::baseline::v2.text:
{
  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "statement": "Chain fresh markdown loss in 2026-Q2 was 4.9% of fresh sales and waste 3.3%, giving total fresh loss of 8.2% — well above the FY26 target of 5.5%.",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-028426f1", "quote": "value: 4.9", "locator": "2026-Q2 markdown_loss_rate, fresh_all"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-620bca08", "quote": "value: 3.3", "locator": "2026-Q2 waste_rate, fresh_all"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-7c1f5264", "quote": "value: 8.2", "locator": "2026-Q2 total_fresh_loss_rate, fresh_all"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-daadf395", "quote": "Reduce total fresh loss from 6.5% to 5.5% of fresh sales by the end of FY26."}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "Dated analytics series updated 2026-07-14, consistent across three metrics for the same period."
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "statement": "The figures quoted in the FY26 objectives document (markdown 4.0%, waste 2.5%) are stale: they match the 2025-Q4 rows, not the latest period.",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-daadf395", "quote": "Fresh markdown losses currently run at 4.0% of fresh sales. Unsold stock written off adds a further 2.5%."},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-7e69b725", "quote": "value: 4.0", "locator": "2025-Q4 markdown_loss_rate, fresh_all"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-0c2d9cde", "quote": "value: 2.5", "locator": "2025-Q4 waste_rate, fresh_all"}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "The prose was written 2026-01-20 and was accurate for 2025-Q4; two quarters have since deteriorated."
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "statement": "On-shelf availability at 17:00 fell to 92.6% in 2026-Q2, below the 93% floor the objectives call the protected measure.",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-1eef13f9", "quote": "value: 92.6", "locator": "2026-Q2 on_shelf_availability_1700, fresh_all"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-daadf395", "quote": "Availability is the protected measure. A loss reduction achieved by ordering less is not a win if availability falls below the floor."}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "Dated metric row against a stated floor. Note the floor is stated 'in every format' but the metric is only published for fresh_all."
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "statement": "Markdown execution compliance and markdown loss move together across formats: neighbourhood stores at 84.6% compliance show 3.9% markdown loss, commuter at 63.2% show 5.5%, high street at 71.4% show 6.2%.",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-51db2484", "quote": "markdown_compliance_pct: 84.6", "locator": "2026-Q2 neighbourhood"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-51db2484", "quote": "markdown_loss_rate_pct: 3.9", "locator": "2026-Q2 neighbourhood"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-ed94d0af", "quote": "markdown_compliance_pct: 63.2", "locator": "2026-Q2 commuter"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-ed94d0af", "quote": "markdown_loss_rate_pct: 5.5", "locator": "2026-Q2 commuter"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-d43ff8d8", "quote": "markdown_compliance_pct: 71.4", "locator": "2026-Q2 high_street"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-d43ff8d8", "quote": "markdown_loss_rate_pct: 6.2", "locator": "2026-Q2 high_street"}
      ],
      "support_level": "moderate",
      "rationale": "The individual figures are facts. The association is cross-sectional across three format aggregates only, and the ordering is not monotonic (high street has higher compliance than commuter but higher loss), so format effects are confounded with compliance."
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "statement": "Compliance is widely dispersed: 137 stores are above 90% and 121 stores are below 40%, giving a large targetable tail within the existing 412-store estate.",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-72006df6", "quote": "note: synthetic - compliance spread is wide: 137 stores are above 90 percent and 121 stores are below 40 percent"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-daadf395", "quote": "Larkfield Markets operates 412 convenience stores in three formats: high street, commuter and neighbourhood."}
      ],
      "support_level": "moderate",
      "rationale": "Stated in the operations export note; the underlying store-level distribution was not supplied, so the tail's format mix and loss rates cannot be checked."
    },
    {
      "id": "C6",
      "statement": "Chain compliance has fallen year on year (78.9% in 2025-Q2 to 75.4% in 2026-Q2) while chain markdown loss rose from 4.2% to 4.9%.",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-307aaf2f", "quote": "markdown_compliance_pct: 78.9", "locator": "2025-Q2 all_formats"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-307aaf2f", "quote": "markdown_loss_rate_pct: 4.2", "locator": "2025-Q2 all_formats"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-72006df6", "quote": "markdown_compliance_pct: 75.4", "locator": "2026-Q2 all_formats"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-72006df6", "quote": "markdown_loss_rate_pct: 4.9", "locator": "2026-Q2 all_formats"}
      ],
      "support_level": "moderate",
      "rationale": "Two matched quarters, same source. Direction is consistent but two data points do not establish causation."
    },
    {
      "id": "C7",
      "statement": "Late execution is stated to raise markdown loss and waste together, not to trade one against the other.",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-883d223e", "quote": "A reduction applied late is a deeper reduction, because the remaining selling window is shorter. Late execution therefore raises markdown loss and waste together rather than trading one against the other."}
      ],
      "support_level": "moderate",
      "rationale": "Category-management statement of mechanism, dated 2026-07-10; no unit-economics or measurement is attached to it in the evidence."
    },
    {
      "id": "C8",
      "statement": "Observation found the 17:00 round starting after 17:30 in four of six stores visited, but the sample was six store-days chosen by a regional manager.",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-e27078c1", "quote": "Observers visited six stores for one trading day each and found that the markdown round started after 17:30 in four of the six."},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-e27078c1", "quote": "The six stores were nominated by the regional manager. They were not selected at random."}
      ],
      "support_level": "low",
      "rationale": "Denominator is six store-days, non-random and hand-picked. Suggestive of a timing problem, not a population estimate."
    },
    {
      "id": "C9",
      "statement": "Intra-day price change is not possible on the current EPOS release, and the release that supports it only enters pilot in Q1 2027 with no committed rollout date.",
      "claim_type": "technical_constraint",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-935708d5", "quote": "The till price file is regenerated once nightly at 02:00. Intra-day price changes are not possible on the current EPOS release."},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-935708d5", "quote": "The EPOS release that supports intra-day price updates enters pilot in Q1 2027. No date is committed for estate-wide rollout."}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "Engineering constraint document dated 2026-06-26."
    },
    {
      "id": "C10",
      "statement": "There is no feed of item-level remaining shelf life, and expiry data exists only where a colleague has scanned at the fixture; an audit of 480 items found 18% with no scanned expiry record.",
      "claim_type": "technical_constraint",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-935708d5", "quote": "There is no supplier or inventory feed of item-level remaining shelf life."},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-935708d5", "quote": "Expiry dates exist only where a colleague has scanned the item at the fixture, and scanning is not enforced."},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-e27078c1", "quote": "A manual audit of 480 items across eight stores found that 18% carried no scanned expiry record at the fixture."}
      ],
      "support_level": "moderate",
      "rationale": "Constraint is documented by engineering; the 18% comes from 480 items in eight stores, so it is indicative of scale, not a chain-level rate."
    },
    {
      "id": "C11",
      "statement": "Both vendor products require inputs Larkfield does not have: Verdant needs ESL plus a real-time till feed; Halcyon needs item-level remaining shelf life from the inventory system.",
      "claim_type": "technical_constraint",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-cb840f63", "quote": "Verdant Pricing Cloud requires electronic shelf labels at the fixture and a real-time price feed from the till system."},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-cb840f63", "quote": "Halcyon Shelf Intelligence consumes item-level remaining shelf life from your inventory system and returns a recommended reduction schedule to the store handheld. No hardware is required."},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-935708d5", "quote": "Electronic shelf labels are fitted in 43 stores. The estate refresh programme reaches a further 77 stores by the end of Q1 2027 and carries the hardware cost centrally, so there is no incremental capital cost to the fresh programme for those stores."}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "Vendor-stated prerequisites read against engineering's documented estate position."
    },
    {
      "id": "C12",
      "statement": "The Verdant 34% markdown reduction figure is a self-selected vendor aggregate with no stated baseline, sample size or method.",
      "claim_type": "stakeholder_opinion",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-cb840f63", "quote": "Verdant Pricing Cloud customers reduce perishable markdown losses by an average of 34%."},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-cb840f63", "quote": "Figures are aggregated from customers who elected to share results with us."}
      ],
      "support_level": "low",
      "rationale": "No denominator, no control, self-selected respondents, reproduced unchecked; procurement notes no contactable reference customer."
    },
    {
      "id": "C13",
      "statement": "FY26 funding is £180,000 of revenue expenditure with no capital, and any programme needing more than £250,000 of capital cannot start before the FY27 planning round in November 2026.",
      "claim_type": "business_constraint",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-2a2e9b15", "quote": "fund the fresh loss programme at £180,000 of revenue expenditure for FY26. No capital allocated."},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-daadf395", "quote": "Any programme requiring more than £250,000 of capital requires board approval and cannot start before the FY27 planning round in November 2026."}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "Finance decision D-2026-02 plus the objectives' capital gate."
    },
    {
      "id": "C14",
      "statement": "No hours may be added to the store labour model, and two-stage markdown was previously rejected on exactly that ground.",
      "claim_type": "business_constraint",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-daadf395", "quote": "Add no hours to the store labour model."},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-2a2e9b15", "quote": "Two-stage markdown was judged to cost colleague hours the estate does not have."}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "Stated objective and prior decision D-2025-11 rationale."
    },
